WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.370
- Oh my God, I cannot do a pull up.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:05.490
Sometimes it's really hard
to find time to work out,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.110
but decades of research shows

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.910
that the stress that
exercise puts on the body

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.700
actually trains it to fend
off major chronic conditions,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.120
like diabetes, cancer,
obesity, and heart disease.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.050
Here we go.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.200
This is hard and it increases
our ability to be independent

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.033
as we age.

00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:21.120
But let's fact check that.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.050
- [Trainer] Go, go, go.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:22.883
You got it.

00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:24.930
- I took two clinical
stress tests. Oh my God.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:28.350
And of course, did lots
and lots of exercise.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.630
The goal was to see how much
I could improve my chances

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.180
of longevity with some
pretty intense workouts.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.200
This is not fun.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.030
It is so hot out here.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:36.982
Let's see how it goes.

00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:38.880
(car whooshing)

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.550
Heather Milton is a clinical
exercise physiologist

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.280
at the NYU Langone
Sports Performance Center

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.740
and for this experiment,
my longevity trainer.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.690
- So we're doing the
stressor of the exercise

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.120
but then allowing you
the adaptation process

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.280
and the lower heart rate in between,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.440
which essentially suppresses stressors.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.400
- [Daniela] This first test

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.840
is going to give us our baseline

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.760
for my longevity-related metrics.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.890
First, we tracked my heart rate recovery

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.350
or how quickly my heart rate
came down from its peak.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.140
For that, we used a
simple heart rate monitor.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.440
The other metric is VO2 max,
which is the maximum rate

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.350
at which your heart, lungs,
and muscles use oxygen.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.960
That was measured with this
sensor that ingested the air

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.850
I was breathing into the tubed mask.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.710
- This is gonna be tighter.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.210
- [Daniela] Oxygen
consumption should be low

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.200
for light walking

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.330
because that shouldn't
really take that much effort.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.480
Every 30 seconds or so, the
treadmill got faster and steeper

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.870
during the test and my
oxygen consumption went up.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.390
- Give it your all. Where are you now?

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.223
All right.

00:01:34.223 --> 00:01:35.070
Almost there. Almost.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.110
- [Daniela] After running
for about nine minutes,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.130
I had to stop.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.490
My heart rate peaked at
167 beats per minute.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.660
- Stay on the treadmill.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.620
It's gonna come down.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.570
I'm gonna watch that heart rate response.

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.690
You currently already have a nice VO2,

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.270
so I wanna see that it's above 26.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.070
Yours was 38.6.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.780
- [Daniela] That's
equal to the milliliters

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.140
of oxygen consumed per kilogram
of your weight each minute.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.380
My heart rate recovery was
only seven beats per minute.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.630
Ideally, it'd be at least 12.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.310
- Recovery is really important

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.880
because that's when we're
allowing our body to process all

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.200
of the stressors that we put in

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.640
and then make the adaptations

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.680
to become bigger, faster, stronger.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:14.970
- [Daniela] On to test number two.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:18.270
I did a series of heavier
and heavier leg presses.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.100
- Press, press, press,
press, press, press, press.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.000
There you go.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.760
- I maxed out at 205 pounds.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.490
That gave us a sense of my
muscle and joint strength,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.920
which directly relates
to having good balance

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.440
and having good balance helps avoid falls,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.770
one of the biggest factors for
preventable deaths as we age.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.300
So with all that info,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.450
Heather put together this
10-day fitness plan for me,

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.880
focusing on cardio and strength training.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.860
I expected a challenge and to sweat.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:44.693
That was hard.

00:02:44.693 --> 00:02:47.310
But even on this short
10-day fitness plan,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.780
I still ran into a few issues

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.921
because the weights I
have are not optimal.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:52.958
(Daniela grunting)

00:02:52.958 --> 00:02:55.197
- I wanna see the video.

00:02:55.197 --> 00:02:56.520
- My GoPro overheated.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.170
86 degrees.

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:00.240
I'm not gonna go outside
because the air quality

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.830
is terrible out there.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.630
I probably should have
picked a better time

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.450
to make this video, like when
I wasn't traveling for work.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.040
- [Captain] We wanna welcome you to Miami.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.530
- But I think that's the
reality for most people.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.650
We squeeze in a workout
whenever we can, and I'm done.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.650
Tomorrow I go back to NYU
for my final stress test.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.680
Hopefully, all of that hard
work over the last 10 days

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:20.970
has paid off.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.273
- All right. Ready to go?

00:03:22.273 --> 00:03:24.330
(Daniela grunting)

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.163
You're in control.

00:03:25.163 --> 00:03:27.450
You tell me when you
absolutely reach that max.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.470
Otherwise, keep going.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.870
You got this. Everything looks great.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.510
- My VO2 max didn't change, as expected.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.820
Heather says, improvements in VO2 max

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.830
take at least six to eight weeks.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.220
It requires a lot of complex
cardiovascular changes,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.500
so things like heart
tissue getting stronger,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.450
lungs taking in more oxygen,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.370
blood vessels becoming more elastic

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.110
and new, healthier red blood cells

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.000
to deliver oxygen to muscles.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.890
We did see other related improvements.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.780
- You're able to do a minute more,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.090
which means you're able
to achieve more work

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:01.020
and a faster pace without
getting to your maximal exertion.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:02.760
- [Daniela] And that meant
my body got more efficient

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:03.720
at using oxygen.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.370
- The higher your fitness level is,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.920
the lower the effort level is

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.790
or the lower the ask for your
body for those general tasks.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.160
- [Daniela] Simple things
like walking around, working,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.750
putting away groceries and cleaning.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.860
- And your heart rate recovery
was much more better too.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.380
- Mine went from seven
to 21 beats per minute.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.850
I was a little less confident though

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.973
about the leg press because
for most of the experiment.

00:04:25.973 --> 00:04:28.080
I mean, I didn't have weights, right?

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.120
I had my two laptops and children.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.460
I can only do five reps of you.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.610
- [Heather] Two, three.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.050
- The first one felt like feathers.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.240
- Yeah, that's a good sign.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.000
- We started at 65 pounds
and went up from there.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.700
I finished the test at 245 pounds,

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.860
40 pounds higher than before.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.580
I guess hard work pays off.

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:49.620
(Daniela chuckling)

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.510
Strength training is also important

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.130
for improving insulin sensitivity

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:55.350
and maintaining healthy
blood glucose levels.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.940
That can stave off some
of those big killers

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.880
I mentioned earlier, things
like diabetes and obesity.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.040
Scientists don't yet know
what the optimal amount

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.470
of exercise for each
person is, but generally,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.280
they recommend 150 minutes of
moderate exercise per week.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:12.000
A 10-year study of 650,000 adults showed

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.040
that those who got at least 75 minutes

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.940
of exercise lived 1.8 years
longer than inactive adults.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.940
People who worked out five to
eight hours a week lived more

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.560
than four years longer.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.420
So I was in that range.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.610
There's no way to say
how many years my 10 days

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.470
of intense workouts added to my life.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.770
But according to this
study, sedentary people age.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:34.410
they did from being
self-sufficient to dependent,

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.060
much sooner than active people.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:37.710
So if I keep up with my workouts,

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.840
I could increase the time
I'm healthy and independent

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.730
by more than a decade.

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:43.253
So what can you do if you
wanna start working out

00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:45.120
towards a healthier, longer life

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.910
but don't have access to
fancy trainers and tests?

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.830
Wearables are a good place to start.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:53.010
They can track metrics like
VO2 max, heart rate recovery

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.500
and walking steadiness pretty accurately.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.150
Heather said that your heart rate data

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:58.590
is the easiest way to go.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.450
Your resting heart rate
should become lower

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.100
as your fitness level improves.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.050
Exercise is generally good for your health

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.450
but too much can also
be counterproductive.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:09.750
For me, the hardest part was
just getting off the couch,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.880
though now that I'm not
getting paid to work out,

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:12.750
it is harder.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.309
So good luck out there.

00:06:14.309 --> 00:06:16.976
(lively music)

